WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.040
- That's some traumatic PTSD right there.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.584
Slamming a book.

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:06.200
(Nicholas laughing)

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.340
It's nice a teacher actually
found a practical use

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.310
for a text book.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.893
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:13.860
- Hi, I'm Nicholas Ferrone.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.060
I'm in my 18th year as
a high school history

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.470
and cultural studies teacher.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.570
- My name is Tselane.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.770
They call me Ms. Tsunami.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:20.770
I'm a teacher.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.680
I've been teaching for
about four plus years,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:23.770
actually five or six years.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:24.603
What am I talking about?

00:00:24.603 --> 00:00:26.020
Time has gone by I'm in the zone.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.120
- I'm in my second year
as a TikTok teacher.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.530
And I only have a TikTok

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.730
because one of my students, Junior Madrid.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:34.570
So shout out to Junior,
encouraged me to get it.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.380
And now I kind of have a
stronger following than he does.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.770
So we have battles back
and forth on TikTok.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.640
- I try to hide my TikTok for my students

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.770
but I love TikTok.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.880
I think it's a great
place to go and dive in

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.330
and get lost in the nonsense.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.873
So first video, let's see.

00:00:49.873 --> 00:00:52.456
(upbeat music)

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.050
This is excessive.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.190
The hair cut and the uniform,

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:13.370
probably happened to him
in the military a lot.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:14.273
Got the book.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.030
That's some traumatic PTSD, right there,

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.623
slamming a book.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.330
It's nice a teacher actually
found a practical use

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.860
for a text book.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.260
That's pretty traumatic.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.240
What I do if a student falls asleep,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.510
is I have everyone in my class
quietly leave the classroom.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.670
And then when they wake up,

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.540
they instantly run out of the classroom

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.030
thinking they're late to their next class.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.920
And we're all in the
hallway just applauding.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.436
- Which one?

00:01:40.436 --> 00:01:42.241
What's the longest river in the world?

00:01:42.241 --> 00:01:44.370
What's the longest river in the world?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.100
- We always try to guide our students.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.850
- It's asking what year
the Louisiana purchase was.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.890
What year?

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.070
What's the largest and deepest ocean?

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:54.913
What's an ocean?

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.819
Now which one is the largest and deepest?

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.409
Think about that.

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:02.900
- Yeah, that's not me during
tests, but I do ask questions.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.120
I won't repeat the same
thing over and over.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.890
'Cause they're just gonna look at me like

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.890
Ms. Tsunami, what are you talking about?

00:02:13.331 --> 00:02:16.600
(Ms Tsunami laughing)

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.690
- I do make a lot of sounds.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:21.250
Or I'll talk to myself,
especially virtual teaching.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.790
I will be talking to myself.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.363
Most teachers either have a teacher look,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.710
a teacher voice which is
what I'm using right now.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.280
Or if you're good, you have both.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.092
I hope I don't make that many sounds.

00:02:34.092 --> 00:02:35.201
(lady screaming)

00:02:35.201 --> 00:02:37.107
- I love these.

00:02:37.107 --> 00:02:38.107
- Oh my God.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:43.280
- Yeah, nobody wants
to wake up in the class

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.850
with their professor all by themselves.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.220
No one, no one, no one I've
had that happen in college.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.053
- I'll be honest.

00:02:48.053 --> 00:02:50.190
I know my student is the
first one to sign on.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.180
They're kind of awkward.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.540
So they'll sign on real quick.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.790
See it's just me and
turn off their camera.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.070
Cause especially if you
didn't like the teacher

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.700
and you didn't have
something to talk about,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.760
they're probably what
are we gonna talk about

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.160
with Mr. Ferrone like
cultural studies or history?

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.545
No we could talk about a lot of stuff.

00:03:03.545 --> 00:03:04.720
- I'm Timothy Chapman.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.070
I'm a third year.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.500
Management and Economics major

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.941
and I'm currently tearing up the slopes

00:03:10.941 --> 00:03:11.960
out here in Colorado with,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.140
- Wow.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:13.973
Wow.

00:03:13.973 --> 00:03:15.968
(Nicholas laughing)

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:18.193
- (indistinct) class today.

00:03:18.193 --> 00:03:23.193
- [Lecturer] Apparently not, have fun.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.800
Try not to catch COVID again,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.540
or for the first time or whatever.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.950
- Can't be mad at him.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.290
- Oh my God.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.310
I am not mad at him.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.270
Listen, I might be teaching
class from Tehidy, I don't know.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.650
- I've had students sit in the
car with me, come from class.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.840
I've had a student in a chair
that they were leaning back

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.340
and I'm like, where are you?

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.740
And they were at the dentist office.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.700
I was flattered that they
did not wanna miss class.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.840
So they were sitting in,
literally dentist chair with me.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.210
I did have a student today
who asked me politely

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.700
if he could leave class
a few minutes early

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.220
'cause he's in
Pennsylvania, paint balling.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:55.060
So that was fine.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:55.900
I thought it'd be funny

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.680
if he actually would have me on camera

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.250
with class while he was running
around playing paintball.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.083
I'm flattered.

00:04:01.083 --> 00:04:01.916
You're at work.

00:04:01.916 --> 00:04:03.896
You're doing something you
still want to be in my class.

00:04:03.896 --> 00:04:04.870
Thank you.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.050
- I have had students doing
classroom from weird locations.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.070
I think a couple of kids
went on vacation to Turkey

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:10.920
or something like that.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.820
And they checked in,
but it looked so crazy.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.533
I was like, wait, wait, where are you?

00:04:20.944 --> 00:04:22.175
Oh, I love that movie.

00:04:22.175 --> 00:04:23.791
That was (indistinct).

00:04:23.791 --> 00:04:24.880
(indistinct) know about that.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.580
(tense music)

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.413
- [Lecturer 2 ] Now, can anyone

00:04:28.413 --> 00:04:29.990
(Ms Tsunami laughing)

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:32.440
- [Lecturer 2] Matt, what
is your roommate doing?

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.160
- I'm more impressed that they know

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.300
the (indistinct) reference.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.780
You'll see people walk behind doing stuff.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.600
I've had a few shirtless parents.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.870
- Yeah, I've had a couple of students

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.110
like try to sneak onto the
camera cause they have pajamas on

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.320
or like they'll do something weird.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.920
And I'm like, I literally see you like.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:50.753
- [Student] Mrs Anderson,

00:04:50.753 --> 00:04:52.522
I know this year has been very challenging

00:04:52.522 --> 00:04:53.830
for all of.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.040
- These videos always get me.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.788
- [Student] So as a class we
wanted to show our appreciation

00:04:57.788 --> 00:04:59.305
and how thankful we are for you.

00:04:59.305 --> 00:05:01.480
You guys can turn on your cameras.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.147
- These are intense.

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:07.500
- Oh your gonna make me cry.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.680
- And again, most people don't realize,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.790
as teachers, we care so
much about what we do.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.350
My students did something
similar this year

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:16.350
and I was so impressed.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.290
Not only that they did it

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.680
but they were able to
coordinate 20 something students

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.750
who normally would not
have their cameras on

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:22.810
and each wrote a letter

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.340
and kind of shared their
love and their gratitude.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.380
I print them out and I
have a little happy folder.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.110
I put all the emails and notes
and letters from students too

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.100
because that's what we look at

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.520
when we're questioning our
existence as educators.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.420
- Okay here we go.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.616
Seriously, is it my fault that
you have your cameras off?

00:05:43.616 --> 00:05:46.004
- [Student 2] So Dr. Brown,

00:05:46.004 --> 00:05:49.883
we actually kind of
wanted to do something.

00:05:49.883 --> 00:05:52.427
- With your, with your cameras off?

00:05:52.427 --> 00:05:55.955
- [Student 2] Everybody
if you wanna go ahead.

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:59.674
(Ms Tsunami laughing)

00:05:59.674 --> 00:06:00.924
- Aww you guys.

00:06:02.301 --> 00:06:04.551
Thank you, very thoughtful.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.270
Hold on just a second,
let me get a Kleenex.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:10.560
- More nice stuff you guys.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.630
The world is already crazy.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.600
So all the nice videos you guys do

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:14.620
with the signs and everything.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:15.780
That's awesome.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.220
I think you guys should
do more of that stuff

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.070
and make more funny videos of course.

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:20.470
- Actually, as I'm looking off screen,

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.600
I have framed a frame.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:23.433
Let me grab it.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.400
This is what we do with
this stuff you give us.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.260
This is a frame post a note

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.550
that one of my students

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.030
who I literally thought hated
my guts the entire year,

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.000
left on my desk at the end of the year.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.240
We battled every single day.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:36.380
And every single day I told her

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:37.380
I'm not gonna give up on you.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.670
And you could yell at me all
you want, but I'm here for you.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:40.940
And I know you're going
through a tough time.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.740
And she was going through
a really tough time.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:43.980
And the last day of school

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.070
when none of my students
were there she was.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:47.590
So as she left, she said,

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.120
I left you something in your desk

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.337
and this is what you left me and said,

00:06:50.337 --> 00:06:52.750
"hi Mr. Ferrone, you're the
best don't ever forget that.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:53.860
You always look out for people.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.570
You're loving, you're caring.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:57.410
And I'm so thankful for you."

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.010
And this is what we do
with the notes you gave us.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.910
So stuff like that is always worth it.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.790
It is never too early to thank a teacher

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.297
who made an impact on your life.

00:07:05.297 --> 00:07:08.047
(soothing music)

